## Intent

Can you compare how our report comment sends the context over, versus lavish-axi? Because as the last review goes, do you really have enough information and context to know where I comment on?
I am not in favor of sending over the png screenshot as it consumes local space as well as more token consumption.
How do we render this in chat? Show an example comparing now, versus how you plan to render it in chat. I feel we can do a much better job in rendering these kind of 1p interaction responses and feedback, and make it feel more native.
adopt-snapdom, and one ship for both (but can be done in separate commits)

## The defect this fixes

The captain's comment t2 on the usage-panel mock (task qd-usage-design-1, rev 1) quoted "under pace: lasts past the reset".
Those words appear twice in that panel, on the 5h row and on the weekly row.
The element was also 0 × 0 when the page opened, because it sits inside the Claude row, which is shut until someone expands it.
The review reached the first mate as only those words and the comment, with the element path and the text either side left in the log.
Firstmate's relay then dropped even the log path.
So the worker could not tell which row was meant, and could not see the place without guessing what to open.

That case is now pinned twice.
A Rust test in `src-tauri/src/review.rs` feeds in the t2 anchor, with its real quote, prefix, suffix and positional path.
It checks that both the first mate's message and the crewmate's relay file carry these lines:
- "match 1 of the 4 places these words appear in the page's text, 2 of them on screen"
- "Usage › Plan limits › Claude › 5h"
- the `div.prov.open[data-prov=claude]` element
- the picture's path

`pnpm artifacts` also picks that cell on a copy of the real mock (`?usage-t2`).

## Why `engine/AGENTS.md` changes

The engine is firstmate's shared material, so this change needs a reason of its own.
Hop two, firstmate retelling the review to the crewmate, is where the anchor was actually lost in t2: the first mate got the words and the log path, and the crewmate got less.
Fixing only what the page captures would have left the lossier hop as it was.
The change is deliberately one line.
It tells firstmate to send the review's relay file to the crewmate unchanged, using the `bin/fm-send.sh` line the review gives, and to put any framing of its own in a separate message.

## The picture: a redraw, sent as a path only

The picture is not a screenshot of what the captain saw.
The page redraws itself with SnapDOM when a place is picked.
Layout and words are right, but images from other sites, canvases and fine detail can differ.
The message to the author says so on every picture line, so no one mistakes it for a photograph.

The captain turned down a PNG in every comment because of disk space and token cost, so:
- Only the file's path goes in the message, about 15 tokens. The JPEG itself is opened only when the author needs it.
- It is a small 1x JPEG, about 11–20 KB for t2.
- It is taken only when the words cannot pin the place down: they repeat on screen, the place is inside something opened, or there are no words there. A Picture toggle in the comment box lets the captain overrule that.
- It is deleted when its draft is taken back, or when its thread is settled while the words are still on the page.

## Two commits, as the captain asked

- `8161f63` covers where a comment sits: the richer anchor, the redrawn picture, the review message and relay file, and the one engine line.
- `b7b565a` shows a sent review in chat as a card that follows it until it is settled, instead of a bubble of the text written for the first mate.
- The later `no-mistakes(...)` commits are the pipeline's fixes to those two.

## What was checked in a browser

- **Browser mock, both themes:** `pnpm artifacts` against the mock, in light and dark, covering the t2 picture in the comment box, the review card when sent, answered and settled, settling from chat, and the t2 card. So did the other page suites: projects, tones, onboarding, attach, usage, routing.
- **chrome-devtools-axi 0.1.35, by hand, both themes:** the chat card. That pass sent a review with no comment, so it showed the card's empty state.
- **Inside the real sandbox, in WebKit as well as Chrome:** the picture was checked in a standalone WKWebView built with Swift, with the app's exact sandbox attributes, both unscrolled and scrolled 300 px. It was correct each time, in 150–200 ms.
- **Not checked:** the Tauri app itself was not run, and its native window was not screenshotted.

## Test-page placeholders changed, then the t2 anchor re-checked

The lint step flagged em dashes on the copied usage-panel test page, used as placeholders for an empty reading at lines 574, 584 and 597.
They are now plain hyphens.
Those lines are in the page's rendering code, while the t2 words are in its data at line 436, which is unchanged.
After the change, `pnpm artifacts` was re-run on the pushed commit (`4a5b57c`) and every t2 check passed: the match count, the nearby labels, the `.prov.open` element, and the picture drawn from inside the sandbox.

## What Changed

- **More detail on where a comment sits.** `review-frame.js` now records more about the spot the captain picked:
  - which match of the quoted words it is, and how many matches are on screen
  - the element's id, classes, data attributes and open states, plus the headings and labels around it
  - its box, and the window size, scroll position and colour scheme it was seen in
- **A picture only when the words can't pin down the spot.** That covers three cases: the words appear more than once, the spot is inside something the captain opened, or there are no words there.
  - In those cases the page redraws itself with SnapDOM 3.1.0 and sends a small cropped JPEG with the spot outlined. SnapDOM is vendored under MIT and served by the app at `artifact://localhost/_qd/snapdom.js`.
  - `review.rs` checks the JPEG and saves it as `review-files/<thread>-r<rev>.jpg`. It deletes the file when a draft is taken back, or when a settled thread's words are still on the page.
  - Only the file path goes into the message, which says the picture is a redraw, not a screenshot.
- **Richer messages to the author.** The review message now lists each part of the location on its own labelled line, with the page's exact file and the picture's path.
  - For a crewmate's page, the same text is also written to `review-files/review-<n>.md`.
  - The message and `engine/AGENTS.md` tell firstmate to forward that file unchanged.
- **Sent reviews show in chat as cards** (`App.tsx`, `styles.css`), replacing the plain text bubble. Each card shows:
  - the verdict and each comment, with the quote it sits on and a mark when a picture went with it
  - any recorded answers, and when the first mate read the review and when the author's task is working on it
  - under each comment, whether a later revision changed or answered it, with actions to open that revision and to settle comments from chat
  - Once every comment is settled, the card shrinks to one line.
  - The review log's `sent` event now keeps the message's first line, and the review summary lists each sent review and its comments. That is how chat finds a card again in a resumed conversation.
- **Test fixture and checks.** A usage-panel fixture page (`?usage-t2`) was added. `pnpm artifacts` checks the picture and the chat card on it, in both themes. The Vite mock serves fixture files with a CORS header, and `AGENTS.md` describes the new checks.

🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)

## Risk Assessment

✅ Low: The only new code since the last review is a one-line change: the summary re-read after a chat-card settle now catches its own error, so the card reports the settle's outcome and not a summary read failure. The earlier fixes checked out as correct on a re-trace: frame-reload clears picks and the pending place, headers are matched uniquely, settle errors show on the card. No new reachable defect turned up in the anchor capture, picture validation and storage, relay file, or chat card paths.

## Testing

<code>I started Vite on its own port and ran `pnpm artifacts` against it with screenshots in both themes: all 238 checks passed. Those checks cover the t2 usage-panel flow: the words that locate a comment, the in-sandbox redraw JPEG sent only when words are not enough, the picture&#39;s review-files path in the message, and the chat review card, including settling from it. I also drove two probes through the UI. In one, the frame reloads while the composer is open: the stale place is dropped, picking again draws a fresh picture, and no page errors appear. In the other, the mock&#39;s reviewSettle and/or reviewSummary is made to fail: a failed settle shows &#34;That did not settle: &lt;settle error&gt;&#34; on the card, and a failed summary re-read alone shows no false error. The Rust review and artifact unit tests pass. Matching a resumed conversation to its reviews after a restart could not be driven, because the mock keeps no state across a reload. I removed a 2 GB `src-tauri/target` left from an earlier round, and the worktree is clean.</code>

