<details>
<summary>🔧 **Review** - 4 issues found → auto-fixed (3) ✅</summary>

- ⚠️ `src/App.tsx:1524` - In a resumed conversation, the chat finds a review by its first line, but that line is not unique. Two reviews of the same page on the same revision with the same verdict get the same header, for example two &#34;Comments only&#34; reviews on rev 1 before the author revises. The header is `Captain&#39;s review of &#34;X&#34; (task Y, rev 1): Comments only.`, written at src-tauri/src/review.rs:828. `byHeader.set` keeps only the last one. After a restart both past captain messages match that review, so the first message&#39;s card shows the second review&#39;s comments, trail and settle buttons, and the error is silent. Fix: keep a list per header and hand them out in order (the nth past message with that header gets the nth sent review), or put something that tells them apart into the header. The same matching applies in the mock (src/host/mock.ts sent header).
- ⚠️ `src/App.tsx:297` - If settling from the chat card fails, the captain is not told. `settleFromChat` settles one thread at a time and only re-reads the summary after all of them succeed. The card&#39;s `settle` (App.tsx:2062) uses try/finally with no catch and is called as `void settle(...)`. If `reviewSettle` rejects partway through &#34;Settle all N&#34; (for example because the home became unreadable), the rejection goes unhandled and nothing appears on screen. The summary is also not refreshed, so threads that were settled before the failure still show as open. Fix: re-read the summary in a finally block and show the error on the card, as the review screen does with `problem`.
- ⚠️ `src-tauri/src/artifact.rs:351` - The new test asserts `REVIEW_FRAME.contains(PICTURE_LIBRARY)`, which only greps the review script&#39;s source for a string. That proves nothing about behaviour: review-frame.js builds the URL from its own literal, and the match would still pass if the line were dead code or commented out. The behaviour is already covered for real by `pnpm artifacts` (?usage-t2), which checks that the picture is drawn from inside the sandbox. Remove this assertion. The status, content-type and served-bytes checks on the served response can stay.
- ℹ️ `src-tauri/src/review-frame.js:57` - If the frame reloads in place, a comment can pick up an old picture. The pick counter lives in the page (`picks`) and starts again at 0 whenever the iframe document reloads. The review screen&#39;s `drawn` map (App.tsx, keyed by pick number) lives in `ArtifactReview`, which is keyed only by revision. When a page reloads within the same revision (for example a form submit, since the sandbox allows forms, or the page navigating itself), the next pick is 1 again. `pictureFor` then finds the earlier `drawn[1]` straight away, and the new comment is saved with a picture of an earlier place. Fix: clear `drawn` and `waitingFor` on `qd:ready` or on the iframe&#39;s `onLoad`.

🔧 Fix applied.
4 issues (2 warnings, 2 infos) still open:

- ⚠️ `src/App.tsx:1524` - In a resumed conversation, the chat finds a review by its first line, but that line is not unique. Two reviews of the same page on the same revision with the same verdict get the same header, for example two &#34;Comments only&#34; reviews on rev 1 before the author revises. The header is `Captain&#39;s review of &#34;X&#34; (task Y, rev 1): Comments only.`, written at src-tauri/src/review.rs:828. `byHeader.set` keeps only the last one. After a restart both past captain messages match that review, so the first message&#39;s card shows the second review&#39;s comments, trail and settle buttons, and the error is silent. Fix: keep a list per header and hand them out in order (the nth past message with that header gets the nth sent review), or put something that tells them apart into the header. The same matching applies in the mock (src/host/mock.ts sent header).
- ⚠️ `src/App.tsx:297` - If settling from the chat card fails, the captain is not told. `settleFromChat` settles one thread at a time and only re-reads the summary after all of them succeed. The card&#39;s `settle` (App.tsx:2062) uses try/finally with no catch and is called as `void settle(...)`. If `reviewSettle` rejects partway through &#34;Settle all N&#34; (for example because the home became unreadable), the rejection goes unhandled and nothing appears on screen. The summary is also not refreshed, so threads that were settled before the failure still show as open. Fix: re-read the summary in a finally block and show the error on the card, as the review screen does with `problem`.
- ℹ️ `src-tauri/src/review-frame.js:57` - If the frame reloads in place, a comment can pick up an old picture. The pick counter lives in the page (`picks`) and starts again at 0 whenever the iframe document reloads. The review screen&#39;s `drawn` map (App.tsx, keyed by pick number) lives in `ArtifactReview`, which is keyed only by revision. When a page reloads within the same revision (for example a form submit, since the sandbox allows forms, or the page navigating itself), the next pick is 1 again. `pictureFor` then finds the earlier `drawn[1]` straight away, and the new comment is saved with a picture of an earlier place. Fix: clear `drawn` and `waitingFor` on `qd:ready` or on the iframe&#39;s `onLoad`.
- ℹ️ `src/App.tsx:2441` - This defect is in the round-1 fix for stale-pick-pictures. `forgetPicks` now runs on the iframe&#39;s `onLoad` and sets `pick` to null, but it leaves `pending` and `withPicture` alone. Suppose a page reloads itself (a form submit, or the page navigating) while the comment composer is open for a place the words cannot pin down, so `withPicture` is true. When the captain saves, `pictureFor` sees `pick === null` and records `{ skipped: &#34;the captain left the picture out&#34; }`. The author is told the captain chose to leave the picture out, which is not what happened. The fix: in `forgetPicks`, also clear `withPicture`, or record a reason that says the page reloaded. Of the two, clearing `pending`, `withPicture` and `draft` so the stale anchor is dropped is the honest option.

🔧 Fix applied.
